---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ScenarioCard from "../components/ScenarioCard.astro";
import { scenarios, scenariosByType } from "../data/scenarios";
---

<BaseLayout title="Troubleshooting" description="Practice fixing real Docker and Kubernetes problems — simulated and on your local cluster.">
  <section class="hero container">
    <span class="badge">Debug like on-call</span>
    <h1>Troubleshooting scenarios</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Six guided scenarios covering Docker crashes, port mapping, CrashLoopBackOff,
      broken Services, ImagePullBackOff, and Pending pods. Practice in the browser first,
      then replay on your local kind cluster.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container prose">
    <h2>Docker scenarios</h2>
    <div class="card-grid">
      {scenariosByType.docker.map((s) => <ScenarioCard scenario={s} />)}
    </div>

    <h2>Kubernetes scenarios</h2>
    <div class="card-grid">
      {scenariosByType.kubernetes.map((s) => <ScenarioCard scenario={s} />)}
    </div>

    <h2>Real cluster practice</h2>
    <p>After the browser simulator, apply broken manifests on your kind cluster:</p>
    <pre class="code-block"><code>{`kubectl create namespace troubleshoot
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=troubleshoot
kubectl apply -f manifests/broken/scenario-01-crashloop.yaml
# Fix it, then try scenario-02 through scenario-05`}</code></pre>
    <p>Full write-up: <code>drills/troubleshooting-scenarios.md</code> in the repo.</p>

    <div class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/practice.html">kubectl simulator</a>
      <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/docker.html">docker simulator</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .code-block {
    background: #0a0e12;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
</style>
